

A B-tree maintains all leaves at the same depth by ensuring that when a node splits, the new node is created at the same level as the original node. For example, if we insert keys 1 to 20 into a B-tree of order 3, the tree will grow in height only when necessary, and all leaves will remain at the same depth. Here’s a step-by-step process:
1. Start with an empty tree.
2. Insert keys 1, 2, 3. The tree now has one node with these keys.
3. Insert key 4. The node splits into two nodes, and a new root is created.
4. Continue inserting keys 5 to 20, ensuring that splits occur at the same level, maintaining the balance of the tree.

What is the worst-case time complexity of a B-tree of order \( n \) with \( m \) nodes?

The worst-case time complexity of a B-tree of order \( n \) with \( m \) nodes is \( O(\log_n m) \). This is because the height of the tree is logarithmic with respect to the number of nodes, and each operation (search, insert, delete) takes time proportional
